a_{n-3}\\), we first need to understand the behavior of the sequence. The characteristic equation for the recurrence relation is derived from the general form \\(a_n = r^n\\), leading to:\n\n\\[\nr^n = 2r^{n-1} - 2r^{n-2} + r^{n-3} \\implies r^3 = 2r^2 - 2r + 1.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nr^3 - 2r^2 + 2r - 1 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe can factorize this polynomial. Testing possible rational roots, we find that \\(r = 1\\) is a root:\n\n\\[\n1^3 - 2 \\cdot 1^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 - 1 = 1 - 2 + 2 - 1 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(r = 1\\) is a root. We can factor \\(r^3 - 2r^2 + 2r - 1\\) as \\((r-1)(r^2 - r + 1) = 0\\). The quadratic \\(r^2 - r + 1 = 0\\) has roots:\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1-4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots are \\(1\\), \\(\\omega = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), and \\(\\omega^2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), where \\(\\omega\\) is a primitive cube root of unity. The general solution to the recurrence relation is:\n\n\\[\na_n = A \\cdot 1^n + B \\omega^n + C \\omega^{2n} = A + B \\omega^n + C \\omega^{2n}.\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\omega^3 = 1\\) and \\(\\omega^6 = 1\\), the terms \\(\\omega^n\\) and \\(\\omega^{2n}\\) are periodic with period 3. Therefore, the sequence \\(a_n\\) is periodic with period 3. To confirm, we can check the values of \\(a_n\\) at \\(n = 20, 25, 30\\):\n\nSince \\(20 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}\\), \\(25 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\\), and \\(30 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\na_{20} = a_2 = 1, \\quad a_{25} = a_1 = 10, \\quad a_{30} = a_0 = 100.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sequence repeats every 3 terms: \\(a_0 = 100\\), \\(a_1 = 10\\), \\(a_2 = 1\\). To find \\(a_{1331}\\), we note that:\n\n\\[\n1331 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(a_{1331} = a_2 = 1\\). The value of \\(a_{1331}\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
